# .gitattributes — line-ending policy for grotap-agents.
#
# Why this exists: this repo is written to concurrently from Windows (local Claude
# sessions) and Linux (the fleet boxes, agent-02..06). With no policy, a Windows
# session commits a file with CRLF and a Linux session commits it back with LF, so
# every edit rewrites EVERY line. agents/lessons/wiring-contracts.md was committed
# CRLF while its seven sibling lesson files were LF: a two-line append then showed
# up as 45 insertions / 42 deletions, and any concurrent edit conflicted on the
# whole file instead of on one hunk. The lesson files take ~80 commits a week from
# three identities, so that is a conflict on nearly every collision.
#
# Deliberately NO `* text=auto`: it guesses text-vs-binary from the first 8000
# bytes and normalizes anything it guesses wrong, which is how a committed PDF got
# corrupted in grotap-platform. Extensions are listed explicitly instead — a file
# type not named here is left completely alone.

*.md      text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.js      text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.html    text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf

# PowerShell is Windows-only tooling: stored LF in the index like everything else,
# but checked out CRLF so it stays conventional for the machine that runs it.
*.ps1     text eol=crlf

# Tracked files with no extension or a bare dotfile name — the globs above match on
# basename, so `*.sh`/`*.json` already cover `.claude-session-init.sh` and `.mcp.json`,
# but these need naming outright. `grotap-git-lock` matters most: it is an
# extensionless bash script with a shebang that runs on the Linux fleet boxes, and a
# CRLF copy of it fails with `bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M`.
grotap-git-lock text eol=lf
CODEOWNERS      text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitkeep        text eol=lf
